## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical prompt governance: how to prove provenance and detect tampering from model transformations in a multi-tenant setting. It tests attack awareness, deterministic auditing, and safe-guard design under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Watermarking robustness under paraphrase/translation
- Non-semantic embedding and tamper detection
- Audit logging for per-tenant prompts
- Edge-case handling and false positives

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal watermark prototype (JS)
function addWatermark(prompt, tag) {
  return prompt + " [WM:" + tag + "]";
}
function detectWatermark(text) {
  const m = text.match(/\[WM:([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\]/);
  return m ? m[1] : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate watermark robustness across languages/models?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic watermark policies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-tenant prompt systems, policy-driven routing, and drift auditing; checks comfort with latency budgeting and audit trails; reveals understanding of safety constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant policy blobs
- Model routing via data_classifications
- Safety guard injection preserving intent
- Drift detection and latency budgeting
- Audit log design and replayability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype illustrating routing and rewriting
function routePrompt(prompt, tenantPolicy, models) {
  // decide avatar
  const avatar = tenantPolicy.data_classifications.includes('medical') ? models.avatarA : models.avatarB;
  // rewrite to inject guard
  const guarded = `Guarded: ${prompt} [guard:${tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.join(',')}]`;
  // fake latency
  return { guarded, avatar: avatar.name };
}
```

